HKMA Supervisory Policy Manual SA-2: Capital Adequacy Requirements

Hong Kong Monetary Authority
Supervisory Policy Manual
SA-2: Capital Adequacy Requirements

1. Introduction
The Hong Kong Monetary Authority (HKMA) requires all authorized institutions to maintain adequate capital to support their risk profile and business activities. This policy manual outlines the minimum capital adequacy requirements for banks operating in Hong Kong.

2. Minimum Capital Requirements
2.1 Common Equity Tier 1 (CET1) Capital Ratio
Authorized institutions must maintain a minimum Common Equity Tier 1 capital ratio of 4.5% of risk-weighted assets. This requirement aligns with the Basel III international standard.

2.2 Tier 1 Capital Ratio
The minimum Tier 1 capital ratio is set at 6.0% of risk-weighted assets. Tier 1 capital consists of CET1 capital and Additional Tier 1 capital instruments.

2.3 Total Capital Ratio
Banks must maintain a minimum total capital ratio of 8.0% of risk-weighted assets. Total capital includes Tier 1 capital and Tier 2 capital.

3. Capital Conservation Buffer
3.1 Buffer Requirement
Authorized institutions are required to maintain a capital conservation buffer of 2.5% of risk-weighted assets, comprising Common Equity Tier 1 capital.

3.2 Buffer Purpose
The buffer is designed to ensure that banks build up capital buffers outside periods of stress which can be drawn down as losses are incurred.

4. Countercyclical Capital Buffer
4.1 Variable Buffer
The HKMA may activate a countercyclical capital buffer ranging from 0% to 2.5% of risk-weighted assets, depending on credit conditions in Hong Kong.

4.2 Implementation
The countercyclical buffer is implemented in accordance with Basel III standards and HKMA's assessment of systemic risk.

5. Leverage Ratio
5.1 Minimum Requirement
Banks must maintain a minimum leverage ratio of 3% of total exposures. The leverage ratio is a non-risk-based measure that supplements risk-based capital requirements.

5.2 Calculation
The leverage ratio is calculated as Tier 1 capital divided by the bank's total exposures, including on-balance sheet exposures and certain off-balance sheet items.

6. Liquidity Requirements
6.1 Liquidity Coverage Ratio (LCR)
Authorized institutions must maintain a Liquidity Coverage Ratio of at least 100%. The LCR ensures that banks have sufficient high-quality liquid assets to withstand a 30-day stress scenario.

6.2 Net Stable Funding Ratio (NSFR)
Banks must maintain a Net Stable Funding Ratio of at least 100%. The NSFR promotes resilience over a longer time horizon by requiring stable funding relative to the liquidity profile of assets.

7. Stress Testing Requirements
7.1 Annual Stress Tests
All authorized institutions must conduct annual stress tests covering credit risk, market risk, and liquidity risk. The stress tests must be approved by the board of directors.

7.2 Scenarios
Stress test scenarios must include severe but plausible adverse economic conditions, including economic downturns, market shocks, and liquidity pressures.

8. Reporting Requirements
8.1 Monthly Returns
Banks must submit monthly returns to the HKMA covering capital adequacy ratios, leverage ratios, and liquidity positions.

8.2 Disclosure Requirements
Authorized institutions must publicly disclose their capital adequacy ratios, leverage ratios, and liquidity coverage ratios on a quarterly basis.

9. Regulatory Capital Instruments
9.1 CET1 Capital
Common Equity Tier 1 capital includes common shares, retained earnings, and other comprehensive income.

9.2 Additional Tier 1 Capital
Additional Tier 1 capital instruments include perpetual non-cumulative preference shares and other instruments that meet specific criteria.

9.3 Tier 2 Capital
Tier 2 capital includes subordinated debt, loan loss reserves, and other instruments that provide loss absorption on a gone-concern basis.

10. Supervisory Review Process
10.1 Individual Capital Guidance
The HKMA may set individual capital guidance above the minimum requirements for institutions based on their risk profile and internal capital adequacy assessment.

10.2 Pillar 2 Requirements
The supervisory review process (Pillar 2) requires banks to have robust internal capital adequacy assessment processes and to hold capital against risks not covered under Pillar 1.

11. Transitional Arrangements
11.1 Phase-in Period
The implementation of Basel III standards in Hong Kong follows transitional arrangements, with full implementation by January 1, 2023.

11.2 Grandfathering
Certain capital instruments issued before specific dates may be grandfathered during the transition period.

12. Enforcement
12.1 Supervisory Actions
The HKMA may take supervisory actions against institutions that fail to meet capital adequacy requirements, including restrictions on dividend payments and business activities.

12.2 Remedial Measures
Institutions with capital deficiencies must submit and implement remedial plans to restore compliance with capital requirements.

Conclusion
The HKMA's capital adequacy framework is designed to ensure the safety and soundness of the Hong Kong banking system. By implementing Basel III standards with appropriate enhancements, the framework promotes financial stability and protects depositors.

Issued by: Hong Kong Monetary Authority
Effective Date: January 1, 2023
Revision: SA-2.5
